  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1230 PM MDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nOur weather pattern hasn't changed much, so our weather hasn't\nchanged much. The upper level ridge continues to be centered near\nthe New Mexico and Arizona border. This position keeps us mostly\ndry with high temperatures well above average. The only area to\nsee a decent chance for rain will be the Gila Region. Like the\nlast few days, thunderstorms are likely to develop this afternoon\nin the higher terrain of the Gila Wilderness and move to the \nsouthwest. There is a good chance we will see another round of \nblowing dust in and around Lordsburg so I have issued a dust \nadvisory there for this afternoon. For Wednesday, we won't see \nmuch of a change in the upper level ridge location, so our weather\nwill again be very similar with a chance for thunderstorms and \nblowing dust west of the Continental Divide and high temperatures \nwell above average. The highs on Wednesday will come down a degree\nor two so the heat advisory has been limited to the Rio Grande \nValley below El Paso.\n\nFor Thursday and Friday the upper level ridge will drift north and\nbe centered near the Four Corners region. This will allow a little\nmore moisture in the region. So we will see at least a slight\nchance for thunderstorms across the most of the area Thursday and\nFriday afternoon and evenings. High temperatures both days will \nstill run 4 to 7 degrees above average. For the weekend, the upper\nlevel ridge to our north will sweep drier air into the region, so\nrain chances will go back down and be mainly limited to the Gila \nRegion. High temperatures will continue to run near the triple \ndigit mark both afternoons. Not much change for the start of next \nweek, so we will continue to see limited afternoon and evening \nthunderstorms with high temperatures running near 100F.",
